<h1><span lang="EN-GB">Point Curvature</span></h1>
<hr align="left" width="98.974%" size="6" class="whs3">
<p class="description"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Description:</b></span> <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point curvature</b></span> is a property of the mesh entities. The curvature at a given point is determined by finding the greatest angle between the normals of the current vertex and the vertices from the chosen neighbourhood level  see the scheme and explanations below for details about neighbourhood levels. Then the result is calibrated to be in the range between 0 and 1. The curvature is then used for determining the strength of the <span class="Command">Reduce Noise By Curvature</span> filter and the amount of selection for the <span class="Command">Select By Curvature</span> <span lang="EN-GB">command.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">Triangles, vertices, and their normals:</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><img src="../images/neighbours.gif" width="499px" height="444px" border="0" class="img_whs4" alt=""></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">There are levels of neighbourhood for each point P.</span></p>
<ul>
<li class="kadov-p-CBullet">
<p class="Bullet"><span lang="EN-GB">Points marked as 1 are neighbours of point P thus defining neighbourhood level 1 for point P.</span></p>
</li>
<li class="kadov-p-CBullet">
<p class="Bullet"><span lang="EN-GB">Points marked as 2 are neighbours of points marked as 1 thus defining neighbourhood level 2 for point P.</span></p>
</li>
<li class="kadov-p-CBullet">
<p class="Bullet"><span><span lang="EN-GB">Points marked as 3 are neighbours of points marked as 2 thus defining neighbourhood level 3 for point P, and so on until the borders of the mesh.</span></span></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">The greater the angle between the normals of two neighbouring points, the greater the curvature. The normals of the points are the average of the normals of their owner triangles.</span></p>
